{
  "narrative_body": "After Russia struck Kyiv with a nuclear-capable Oreshnik missile, Macron called Alexander Lukashenko, Belarus's president — the first conversation between the two men since February 2022, and a signal that France is trying to fracture Russia's alliance system rather than merely condemn its escalation.\n\nThe call was at Macron's initiative. He carried two messages: a warning against Belarus following Russia deeper into its war of aggression, and an invitation for Minsk to approach Europe. The context made the timing deliberate. Zelensky had flagged Belarus as a potential new Russian strike vector; Lukashenko has been releasing political prisoners and sending signals westward. Russia's Oreshnik barrage — 600 drones, 90 missiles, 4 dead in Kyiv — gave the moment urgency. France read the escalation as a crack to lever open. Macron condemned the strikes as a dead end, but his substantive move was the phone call to Minsk.\n\nThat same week, Jean-Noël Barrot, the foreign minister, banned Itamar Ben-Gvir from France, citing what he called his \"unspeakable actions\" against French and European nationals on the Gaza flotilla. France summoned the Israeli ambassador and — with Italy — called for EU-level sanctions against the Israeli minister. Barrot also criticised the flotilla itself as producing \"no useful effect,\" maintaining France's characteristic dual stance: pressure on Israel's far right without full alignment with the pro-Palestinian camp. Thirty-six French nationals were aboard the vessel.\n\nThe week's most consequential story, though, was domestic. What had been described as cronyism is now documented as a strategy. Parliament confirmed Emmanuel Moulin as governor of the Banque de France on May 20, despite 54% of MPs voting against him — the 3/5 threshold for a veto was not met. Moulin is a former Élysée secretary-general and a Macron confidant. But Mediapart and Le JDD documented that this appointment is the capstone of something larger: more than 20 former Macronist figures placed in key state positions in recent months. Richard Ferrand, former president of the National Assembly, is now at the Conseil Constitutionnel. Amélie de Montchalin, a former minister, went to the Cour des Comptes. Marc Guillaume, a former government secretary-general, is at the Conseil d'État. Baptiste Rossi, Macron's speechwriter, went to France Télévisions. Europe 1 sourced the operational logic: to prevent the National Rally from governing if it takes power in 2027. Ms Le Pen put it bluntly: Macron is \"multiplying appointments of friends to obstruct the RN after 2027.\" A constitutional law professor at the Sorbonne raised democratic legitimacy concerns. The placements are legal. Their scale and timing — twelve months before the end of Macron's mandate — make them something more than ordinary patronage.\n\nThe party Macron is trying to contain is showing its own fractures. Marine Le Pen and Jordan Bardella, the RN president, contradicted each other publicly on their flagship promise to lower the retirement age to 62. Mr Bardella told German press the question was \"being examined.\" Ms Le Pen publicly held the commitment. Libération noted drily that \"the RN still doesn't understand its own position on pensions.\" The divergence runs deeper: a social-populist wing loyal to Le Pen defends welfare-state positions, while a business-friendly camp around Bardella wants to revise the programme. The Engie chief executive made the business community's view plain, saying RN ideas are \"bad for France, for energy security, for electricity prices and decarbonisation.\" The July 7 appeal verdict is forcing the split into the open — RN cannot maintain programmatic ambiguity while simultaneously preparing two distinct 2027 campaign profiles.\n\nInto this fragmented landscape, Gabriel Attal, a former prime minister, declared his 2027 candidacy on May 22 at a citizen debate in Mur-de-Barrez, a village in Aveyron — deliberately rural, deliberately distant from the Macron urban-centrist legacy he is trying to both inherit and escape. He also took over Macron's 2022 campaign social media accounts, some 85,000 followers, and renamed them \"Attal président,\" causing friction with Renaissance and drawing criticism from Ms Le Pen. The RN predicted the candidacy would last \"until autumn.\"\n\nOn the fiscal front, Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu put a number on the strain. The Middle East war has already cost France more than €6 billion. At a Matignon press conference flanked by 10 ministers — a deliberate signal of collective ownership — Mr Lecornu unveiled €710 million in fuel subsidies targeting workers, fishers, farmers, and home-care workers, raising the employer fuel supplement cap from €300 to €600. He ruled out tax cuts and promised new spending cuts by mid-June. \"This war will last,\" he said, \"with no return to normal before somewhere between summer and autumn.\" The mid-June budget review is the next material watch point.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Lecornu announces €710M fuel aid package as Middle East war drives prolonged energy crisis",
      "summary": "Prime Minister Lecornu held his first press conference at Matignon on 21 May, unveiling €710M in targeted fuel subsidies for workers, taxi drivers, fishing fleets, and home-care workers, citing the ongoing US-Israel-Iran war and Strait of Hormuz blockade. He refused a broad tax cut on fuel, warned the conflict 'will last,' and said the total fiscal cost — already €6B+ — will require new budget cuts to be identified by mid-June."
    },
    {
      "headline": "RN internal tensions over 2027 programme: Le Pen and Bardella diverge on pension reform age",
      "summary": "Le Pen and Bardella gave contradictory signals on the RN's flagship promise to lower retirement age to 62: Bardella told German press the question was 'being examined,' while Le Pen publicly maintained the 62-year commitment. Multiple outlets reported this as symptomatic of a broader strategic fracture between a social-populist camp loyal to Le Pen and a more business-oriented current around Bardella, with the July 7 appeal verdict on Le Pen's eligibility adding urgency."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Macron names ally Emmanuel Moulin as Banque de France governor, drawing opposition from across parties",
      "summary": "Parliament validated the nomination of Emmanuel Moulin — former Élysée Secretary-General and Macron confidant — as governor of the Banque de France on 20 May, despite over 54% of parliamentarians voting against him (3/5 threshold required for veto). The appointment triggered a broader controversy about Macron systematically installing loyalists (also including Richard Ferrand at the Conseil Constitutionnel, Amélie de Montchalin at the Cour des Comptes, and Macron's speechwriter Baptiste Rossi at France Télévisions) in key state positions ahead of the 2027 election."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bardella's girlfriend at centre of media storm: Bourbon des Deux-Siciles family sues Belgian outlet for defamation",
      "summary": "Following Jordan Bardella's public disclosure of his relationship with aristocrat Maria Carolina de Bourbon des Deux-Siciles (via Paris Match cover in April), media scrutiny intensified over her family's ties to a $600M inheritance dispute linked to her grandfather's alleged corruption. The family filed a defamation suit against Belgian outlet DHnet. Bardella's own preliminary presidential campaign was cited as the underlying target."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Macron slavery reparations ceremony: supports Code Noir repeal, opens debate on reparations",
      "summary": "At a ceremony marking the 25th anniversary of the Loi Taubira recognising the slave trade as a crime against humanity, Macron on 21 May supported parliament's bill to symbolically repeal the Code Noir and for the first time evoked the 'immense question' of reparations — without making concrete commitments. He also announced a joint Franco-Ghanaian scientific commission on the subject."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gabriel Attal officially declares 2027 presidential candidacy, inherits Macron's 2022 social media accounts",
      "summary": "Former Prime Minister Gabriel Attal formally declared his candidacy for the 2027 presidential election on 22 May at a citizen debate in Mur-de-Barrez (Aveyron). It also emerged that Attal had taken over and rebranded Macron's 2022 campaign social media accounts (some 85,000 followers) to 'Attal président,' causing friction with Renaissance and provoking criticism from Marine Le Pen."
    }
  ]
}

